 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Supply and common mode",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The sensed common-mode voltage at RS+ and the supply at VCC each run from 4.5 V to 76 V, independently of each other: VCC may sit below the sensed rail, and a battery down to 4.5 V can be monitored. Sensing in the high side leaves the load's ground return untouched."
  },
  {
   "topic": "MAX4080 signal path",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Load current through RSENSE produces VSENSE. The inverting input of the internal amplifier A1 draws almost no current, so it sits at the source voltage less ILOAD × RSENSE; A1 drives transistor Q1 until the drop across RG1 equals VSENSE. A current mirror scales the RG1 current by β; A2 turns that into a voltage and A3 buffers it to OUT. Overall gain is 5 V/V (F), 20 V/V (T) or 60 V/V (S). The MAX4080 senses one direction only."
  },
  {
   "topic": "MAX4081 signal path",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The MAX4081 input stage has two sides, Q1/RG1 and Q2/RG2, each with its own current mirror, so current through RSENSE in either direction is matched by A1. A2 then shifts the result to VREF through two 125 kΩ resistors from REF1B and REF1A. OUT rises above VREF when RS+ is above RS- (battery charging) and falls below it for the opposite direction; with no sense voltage OUT equals VREF. One output therefore carries both magnitude and direction."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gain resistors",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "RG1 and RG2 are 160 kΩ in the F version, 60 kΩ in the T and 20 kΩ in the S."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reference inputs (MAX4081)",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "REF1A and REF1B set VREF, their average. Either tie both to a quiet, regulated reference, so OUT equals that voltage at zero current, or ground REF1B and drive REF1A, so OUT rests at half the REF1A voltage. In both modes OUT goes above VREF for VRS+ > VRS- and below it for VRS+ < VRS-. The tied range is 1.5 V to 6 V; REF1A alone, with REF1B grounded, takes 3 V to 12 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Choosing gain and sense resistor",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Ideally the largest load current gives the full-scale sense voltage: 1000 mV for the 5 V/V part, 250 mV for 20 V/V, 100 mV for 60 V/V. Pick the gain that gives the output swing the design needs. A larger RSENSE costs supply voltage and I²R heating but makes offset a smaller share of the reading, so small currents read more accurately. The resistor must handle its own dissipation, or its value drifts or it fails. Where the current has fast edges keep the resistor's inductance low: wire-wound types have the most, metal film less, and low-inductance types built as a straight metal band come in values under 1 Ω."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output range of the MAX4081",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Although the input accepts either polarity equally, the room for the output above VREF (up to VCC) is usually larger than below it (down to GND), unless VCC is near its minimum. Pick VCC and VREF so the swing each way covers the current range in that direction without clipping."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypassing",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "A 0.1 µF ceramic capacitor from VCC to GND suits most designs. VCC may be tied to RS+ or RS-, or fed from a separate regulated supply. Where VCC sees fast line transients, a series resistor ahead of the bypass capacitor slows them and reduces the disturbance at OUT; 1 kΩ with 0.1 µF suits most cases."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The part has no special layout or grounding needs of its own. The sense path does: stray trace resistance in series with RSENSE adds error, so use a four-terminal resistor or Kelvin connections, and keep the heavy battery currents from upsetting the measurement."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overdrive",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The output does not reverse phase when the inputs are overdriven; it recovers from saturation in about 50 µs. OUT is clamped inside to no more than 18 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Process",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Bipolar."
  }
 ],
